Give the IP24 machines an explicit device-interleaving quantum.

REQUIRED PAIR with mame-pit8253-idle-strobe-rearm.patch. Do not apply one
without the other, in either direction. The PIT patch is a pure win on its own
terms — it deletes 1.67 million dead timer callbacks per emulated second — but
those callbacks were also, accidentally, the only thing interleaving this
driver's CPU with the rest of its machine, and deleting them without putting a
quantum in their place BREAKS THE GUEST.

WHAT BREAKS
-----------
MAME ends a timeslice at the earlier of the next timer expiry and the scheduling
quantum. `ip24_base()` never called `set_maximum_quantum()`, so the machine ran
on MAME's 1/60 s default -- and got away with it only because the idle 8254
re-armed an update timer twice per microsecond, which ended every timeslice long
before the quantum could. Cycles per `execute_run()` were 88.9.

Remove the storm and that number rises to ~673, the IOC2 keyboard controller and
the main CPU start running in long alternating blocks, and IRIX's PS/2
diagnostic fails outright:

    PC keyboard/mouse controller diagnostic    *FAILED*
            Check or replace:  CPU base board
    Cannot connect to keyboard -- check the cable.
    Cannot open keyboard() for input

The desktop still comes up. It has no keyboard and no mouse. Verified from a
real framebuffer screendump, not from a log.

Note the DIRECTION of this change. 8 us is far finer than the 16.67 ms MAME
would otherwise use here, so an explicit quantum on this driver TIGHTENS device
interleaving relative to stock MAME rather than relaxing it. It is closer to the
storm's own ~0.5 us cadence than the default ever was.

WHY 8 us, AND THE CLIFF ABOVE IT
--------------------------------
There is a hard correctness boundary between 16 and 32 microseconds. Cold boots
of golden v3 on `indy_4610`, one binary with the quantum set from the
environment so nothing but the knob differs, scored as "reached the 4Dwm
desktop" from framebuffer signatures:

    quantum      boots OK
      4 us         2 / 2
      8 us         2 / 2
     16 us         4 / 4
     32 us         0 / 4      black framebuffer, never recovers
     64 us         0 / 1      "
    128 us         0 / 1      "
    default        0 / 1      keyboard/mouse diagnostic FAILED (above)

8 of 8 below the boundary, 0 of 6 above it (Fisher exact p ~ 3e-4). The known
background rate of that black cold-boot hang on this image is 2 in 10, so the
failures above 32 us are not it.

8 us is therefore chosen as the largest value with MORE THAN ONE OCTAVE of
margin below a measured cliff. 16 us is faster in the measurements taken and
would have been the tempting choice; it sits exactly one octave under a wall,
and this driver's failure mode at the wall is a guest that never finishes
booting.

WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED
-----------------------
The SPEED difference between 4, 8 and 16 us is NOT established. Those runs were
taken on a box carrying 47-82% foreign CPU on the claimed core pair, and the
apparent ordering tracks the contention rather than the knob. Re-measure that on
a quiet box before treating 8 us as final; it is the one number between this
patch and promoting a binary to the tile.

diff --git a/src/mame/sgi/indy_indigo2.cpp b/src/mame/sgi/indy_indigo2.cpp
--- a/src/mame/sgi/indy_indigo2.cpp
+++ b/src/mame/sgi/indy_indigo2.cpp
@@ -298,6 +298,16 @@
 
 void ip24_state::ip24_base(machine_config &config, uint32_t system_clock)
 {
+	// Explicit device-interleaving quantum. Until the 8254 idle re-arm storm was
+	// fixed (mame-pit8253-idle-strobe-rearm.patch) this driver had one by
+	// accident: an idle counter re-armed its update timer twice per microsecond,
+	// and MAME ends a timeslice at the next timer expiry. With that gone the
+	// machine falls back to MAME's 1/60 s default, the IOC2 keyboard controller
+	// is no longer interleaved finely enough, and IRIX's PS/2 diagnostic fails
+	// with "Cannot connect to keyboard". Boots break outright at 32 us and
+	// above; 8 us keeps more than an octave of margin under that.
+	config.set_maximum_quantum(attotime::from_usec(8));
+
 	SGI_MC(config, m_mem_ctrl, m_maincpu, m_eeprom, system_clock);
 	m_mem_ctrl->int_dma_done_cb().set(m_ioc2, FUNC(ioc2_device::mc_dma_done_w));
 	m_mem_ctrl->eisa_present().set_constant(1);
